92186055535 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055535 #edb/ref 1061 13784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055535 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a8a7097-d5fe-52e6-b191-a54ff1ff6ee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055535 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055536 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055536 #edb/ref 1061 13785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055536 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d9fa15b-505e-5efb-acca-ed49130fabb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055536 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055537 #edb/ref 1061 13932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055537 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5aabf84e-eb5f-5695-bef0-ba3fb10691b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055537 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055965 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & =-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the fourth power, we return to the value of $1,$ which can also be written as $\\textrm{i}^0 = 1.$ This means that if we continue to increase the powers, we would cycle through the same four values once again, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3f3a42dd591daf109e474eec99e446706e0d8e46f1252540839cd50d957463.png)\n\nSo, for $\\textrm{i}^5$ through $\\textrm{i}^8,$ the exact same pattern repeats. Remembering that $\\textrm{i}^4 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^5&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^6&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^7&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^8&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 .\n\t}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $\\textrm{i}^9$ through to $\\textrm{i}^{12},$ the pattern repeats again:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^9&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{10}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{11}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\textrm{i}^{12}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 \n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to simplify a higher integer power of $\\textrm{i},$ we *divide the power by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder*.\n\nLet's see this idea in action." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055965 #edb/ref 1065 2762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055965 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "be735724-c1e7-5d1c-b396-61d58ee81903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055965 #edb/ref 1067 "The Cyclic Nature of Powers of i" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055966 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's compute the first few powers of $\\textrm{i},$ where $\\textrm{i} = \\sqrt{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use these results to evaluate expressions containing powers of $\\textrm{i}.$ Let's take a look at some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055966 #edb/ref 1065 2763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055966 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6fe2451a-f8be-5178-a84f-66817536929d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055966 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234139 #edb/ref 1069 "q-533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234139 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the value of ${\\text{i}}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ecda4efc-f543-5a75-8638-7f985bba9d1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234139 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the first few powers of $\\text{i},$ where $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{i}}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{1} & =\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{3} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{i}=(-1)\\cdot \\text{i}=-\\text{i} \\\\ {\\text{i}}^{4} & ={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514874 #edb/ref 1080 "-5\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514874 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514874 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00d2f4f8-927e-5d9b-a303-a838ea7066a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514886 #edb/ref 1080 "-15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514886 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514886 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05e86823-606d-52b9-b22c-08dc8213a50f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514901 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514901 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514901 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0df1ec4e-edbd-5130-a905-b8d2bac5e501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514911 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "120a71cf-8528-512a-82ff-1d95f599c730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514918 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514918 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514918 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "150744f4-cb82-55ac-ae42-7e7c6a5fee3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514922 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514922 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514922 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17650096-45ec-54b0-9f2b-20816e3ea300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514923 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514923 #edb/re